  "id": "COMPOUND:537 - PROTEIN:1380",
  "verbalization": "Acetohydroxamic acid is implicated in the relationship with ATP synthase subunit a through multiple pathways, suggesting a potential role in modulating mitochondrial function. The evidence indicates that acetohydroxamic acid acts as a target for several entities, including 72 kDa type IV collagenase and 6-hydroxy-2-(hydroxymethyl)-1-methoxy-3-[(2S,3R,4S,5S,6R)-3,4,5-trihydroxy-6-(hydroxymethyl)oxan-2-yl]oxyanthracene-9,10-dione, all of which are targets of ATP synthase subunit a. This suggests that acetohydroxamic acid may influence the activity of ATP synthase subunit a through a series of intermediate molecular interactions. The presence of multiple distinct targets for acetohydroxamic acid, each leading to the same final target, supports the idea that it may exert its effects through different mechanisms that ultimately converge on ATP synthase subunit a. The integration of these pathways highlights the potential for acetohydroxamic acid to modulate mitochondrial ATP production, which could have implications for cellular energy homeostasis. The evidence is consistent with a mechanism involving the inhibition or regulation of ATP synthase subunit a, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
